# JARVIS cron jobs for the `jarvis` user.  Install:  crontab deploy/crontab      (server clock must be UTC)
# Times are UTC.  NSE session is 03:45-10:00; the equity live feed runs as the jarvis-feed service, not from
# cron.  The futures/options feed (below) runs once a day from cron instead, by design -- see section 17.
SHELL=/bin/bash
PATH=/home/jarvis/JARVIS/.venv/bin:/usr/local/bin:/usr/bin:/bin
JARVIS=/home/jarvis/JARVIS

# No daily Upstox token refresh job here: UPSTOX_FEED_TOKEN should be the Analytics Token (1-year
# validity, generated by hand once at the developer console, confirmed with `auth check`, section
# 13.2) rather than the daily OAuth login, which needs a human in a browser and can't be cron'd anyway.

# Daily OHLC for every market (India plus the index universe from `python -m data_layer.index_universe build`), after the last close
# (NYSE closes 21:00 UTC in winter).  It runs an hour after the 1-minute bars job below because both take the scheduler lock: with about
# two thousand tickers the bars job can take 20 to 30 minutes, and a second scheduler that finds the lock held exits.
0 23 * * 1-5   cd $JARVIS && python scheduler.py --job ohlc-daily  >> logs/scheduler.log 2>&1
# NSE index members and weights (SCRUM-183): member lists of NIFTY 50, BANK, FIN, NEXT 50, MIDCAP SELECT, the monthly factsheets' top ten
# and approximate weights from the Yahoo shares and the day's close, so after the daily OHLC job above.  A snapshot is stored only when
# something changed; about 10 requests.
50 23 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.index_weights refresh >> logs/index-weights.log 2>&1
# Intraday 1-minute bars for every market but India (US, UK, EU, JP, KR, TW, SG, CN, HK) from Yahoo, once after the last close.  Each run re-fetches the last 5 days
# (idempotent) and also looks for weekdays in the last 28 with no rows for a market (see data_layer/gaps.py): those are fetched back to the oldest one, since
# Yahoo keeps only 30 days of 1-minute bars.  A weekday still empty after two nights is taken to be a holiday.  India has its own job above.
0 22 * * 1-5   cd $JARVIS && python scheduler.py --job bars --market ALL --interval 1m >> logs/scheduler.log 2>&1
# India 1-minute candles from Upstox (the feed stores ticks; this keeps the minute-candle history, which the viewer's History tab and
# the research reads, current).  After the close and the 10:05 compaction.  It looks at the last 14 days against the NSE holiday calendar and fetches
# only the open days with no India rows: about 500 calls (10 to 15 minutes) when yesterday is missing, none when nothing is.  A day that stays empty
# for two nights is taken to be a special holiday and not asked for again.  The 400 calls per 30 minutes leave room for the long history backfill
# (1,500) and the 12:50 fundamentals job inside Upstox's 2,000, while the backfill is still running.
30 10 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.upstox.backfill recent --budget-30min 400 >> logs/backfill-recent.log 2>&1
# NSE participant-wise open interest and volume (Client, DII, FII, Pro; SCRUM-175): two ~1 KB files a day from NSE's public archive, published
# in the IST evening.  Looks at the last 10 weekdays, so a file NSE posts late is picked up the next night; a day that stays missing is a holiday.
# The history from 2012 is a one-off:  python -m data_layer.participant_oi backfill --from 2012-01-01   (about 7,500 requests at 1 a second)
10 16 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.participant_oi daily --days 10 >> logs/participant-oi.log 2>&1
# NSE F&O bhavcopy (every futures and options contract's close, settlement, OI, volume; SCRUM-184): one zip a day (0.3-1.7 MB stored), published
# in the IST evening.  Looks at the last 10 weekdays like the job above.  History from 2015 is a one-off (about 3,000 requests, ~2.5 GB):
#   python -m data_layer.eod_fo backfill --from 2015-01-01
25 16 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.eod_fo daily --days 10 >> logs/eod-fo.log 2>&1
# NSE FII derivative statistics (FII buy, sell and OI in contracts and crores; SCRUM-185): one ~10 KB .xls a day, needs xlrd.  The files start
# on 2014-12-01; that history is a one-off:  python -m data_layer.fii_derivatives backfill --from 2014-12-01   (about 3,100 requests)
35 16 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.fii_derivatives daily --days 10 >> logs/fii-derivatives.log 2>&1
# NSE daily market files (SCRUM-187): delivery, F&O ban list, combined OI / MWPL, short selling (dated archive files, last 10 weekdays),
# and the last session's bulk and block deals (NSE keeps no dated archive of those: history only builds forward, or by hand with
# `import bulk_deals FILE`).  The ban list for the next session appears in the IST evening, so this runs after the files above.
# History is a one-off:  python -m data_layer.nse_daily_files backfill --from 2015-01-01   (about 10,000 requests over the four dated files)
45 16 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.nse_daily_files daily --days 10 >> logs/nse-daily-files.log 2>&1
# Daily FX rates from Yahoo (for comparing earnings in one currency with prices in another); after the Asian and European closes
30 0 * * *     cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.fx refresh >> logs/fx.log 2>&1
# Money-market rates (SCRUM-186): one request to the RBI home page for the repo rate and corridor, call money range and T-bill cut-offs.
# History builds forward; overnight MIBOR (FBIL publishes it only inside its web app) is loaded by hand:  python -m data_layer.rates import FILE
40 12 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.rates daily >> logs/rates.log 2>&1
# Fundamentals from Yahoo (decision SCRUM-138): a daily slice, never-fetched and failed symbols first, then symbols
# with a missing year (retried weekly), then the oldest.  What is due comes from what is stored, so a day the job did not run is caught up
# by the next one (--catch-up).  Then the pillar inputs (ROIC, debt, FCF in the price's currency) and the four-year completeness check.
0 1 * * *      cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.yf_fundamentals run --limit 150 --catch-up 400 >> logs/yf-fundamentals.log 2>&1
0 2 * * *      cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.yf_pillars build >> logs/yf-pillars.log 2>&1 && python -m data_layer.yf_fundamentals check >> logs/yf-completeness.log 2>&1
# Nightly backup snapshot: parked until a separate disk or bucket exists (SCRUM-121); health only warns, cron_check does not require it.
# When it is set up (JARVIS_BACKUP_DIR in .env), add:  30 23 * * *  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.backup run >> logs/backup.log 2>&1
# Research snapshot (research/snapshot.py): hard links to the lake's closed files, so the read-only research user never opens a file the
# collector can still change.  After the end-of-day health report (11:30) and the 10:05 compaction; nice and ionice keep it out of the way.
# Needs the research root on the lake's filesystem: run deploy/research/setup.sh (it prints what it would do first).  Pruning removes only
# old snapshot links, never lake files; each kept snapshot costs only the files that were rewritten since (about 50 MB).
45 11 * * *    cd $JARVIS && nice -n 19 ionice -c3 python -m research.snapshot create --keep 45 >> logs/research-snapshot.log 2>&1
# Market calendar: Upstox's holiday list, so every check knows whether a day should have produced data (before the 03:15 health report)
0 3 * * *      cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.market_calendar refresh >> logs/market-calendar.log 2>&1
# Morning health report, before the session; mail or read logs/health.log
15 3 * * 1-5   cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.health >> logs/health.log 2>&1
# End-of-day health report, after the feed has compacted the day (10:05 UTC) and Yahoo's daily bar exists
30 11 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.health --after-close >> logs/health.log 2>&1

# Futures and options (NSE_FO; runbook section 17): refresh the instrument master and roll the tracked
# watchlist (data/fo_watchlist.csv: 2 futures and 2 option expiries per index, all strikes) to today's nearest
# expiries before the session opens.  The feed refuses to start if the universe file is not from today or has fewer
# expiries than the watchlist asks for (--allow-stale-universe overrides it).
# What is stored comes from .env, read by both the equity service and this job (production, from 2026-11-03):
#   JARVIS_TICK_STORE=full  JARVIS_EXT_FIELDS=1  JARVIS_GREEKS=core  JARVIS_SYSMON=1
# core keeps IV, delta and gamma; theta, vega and rho are computed by the viewer (data_layer/greeks_calc.py).
# JARVIS_SYSMON=1 writes CPU, memory and disk samples to logs/resources-<segment>-<date>.jsonl.
25 3 * * 1-5   cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.upstox.fo_instruments import --download >> logs/fo-select.log 2>&1 && python -m data_layer.upstox.fo_instruments select --watchlist data/fo_watchlist.csv >> logs/fo-select.log 2>&1
# Run one full fo session (same 03:40 UTC start as the equity service). --once exits once that day's
# session closes and is compacted, so cron runs it fresh every weekday -- this is how the roll above
# takes effect, since a long-lived process would keep the contract list it started with (section 17.6).
40 3 * * 1-5   cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.upstox.feed --once --segment fo >> logs/fo-feed.log 2>&1

# Upstox company fundamentals (SCRUM-103).  Key ratios are today's value only, so a daily snapshot builds their history.
# Statements, shareholding, corporate actions, profile and peers: a weekly pass (Sunday 13:00 UTC, outside the feed window) that skips companies fetched in the last
# 20 days (about monthly; a full pass over 500 companies is roughly 90 minutes at the Upstox rate limit).
50 12 * * 1-5  cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.upstox.fundamentals run --set daily >> logs/fundamentals.log 2>&1
0 13 * * 0     cd $JARVIS && python -m data_layer.upstox.fundamentals run --set full --max-age-days 20 >> logs/fundamentals.log 2>&1
